WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.280
A practical network hardening routine starts with inventory and ownership.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:10.180
Know the assets, subnets, VLANs, cloud networks, wireless networks, services,

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.480
management interfaces, third-party connections, and critical dependencies.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.380
Assign owners so hardening decisions have context and accountability.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.880
Map the paths that matter most, including user access, application dependencies,

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:31.460
administrative access, DNS, DHCP, NTP, monitoring, backup, and remote access.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.320
Next, reduce unnecessary exposure.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:40.780
Disable or retire what is not needed, remove obsolete rules, restrict public exposure,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.200
protect management interfaces, and document exceptions.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.060
Segment networks by purpose, risk,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.480
and sensitivity so compromise or failure in one area does not automatically affect every

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.740
other area.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:58.200
Keep segmentation understandable enough that teams can troubleshoot and maintain it.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.220
Then protect administration and harden devices.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.440
Use unique accounts, least privilege, multi-factor authentication,

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.780
controlled access paths, management networks, bastion hosts where appropriate, logging,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.920
and protected emergency access.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.580
Apply approved baselines to routers, switches, firewalls, wireless controllers,

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:24.160
VPN gateways, DNS, DHCP, NTP, and other shared services.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.260
Back up configurations and stage changes carefully.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:32.120
Finally, patch, monitor, validate, and improve.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:37.940
Use maintenance windows, testing, change approval, backups, and rollback plans.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.160
Track exceptions and review them.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:47.660
Monitor logs, alerts, configuration drift, rule sets, access, availability, DNS,

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.200
certificates, and failed changes.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:55.300
Network hardening is practical risk reduction through disciplined operations: inventory,

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:02.720
classify, reduce exposure, segment, control access, patch, monitor, validate, and improve.
